**English Rewrite**

Three hours after getting off work, I went home and went online, and saw people mourning #YuYing-shih. At the time, all I knew was that he was the idol of the famous blogger Professor #WangWei-hsiung; beyond that, I knew nothing about his life.

But in the 21st century you don't have to consult #teachers for everything—with Google you can know everything under heaven. After looking things up for a while, I increasingly understood why he was so famous, and why Professor Wang liked to bring up Old Yu so much…

Old Yu was a professor at three Ivy League schools, you know! Isn't that impressive! Just dropping his name is enough to scare people to death!

I was like a stalker, constantly digging up background information on Yu Ying-shih: what Chinese Wikipedia said about him, what English Wikipedia said about him, how people praised him when he won awards, and even what today's obituaries said about him. A person I respect the most surfaced in my mind… Teacher Yungen. They really are quite similar!

It's a pity Teacher Gen didn't have a Harvard teaching position, but their traditional Chinese kung fu of #being well-versed in the past and present and #I am Huaxia is certainly cut from the same cloth. Teacher Gen liked Daoism and Buddhism; Old Yu seems to have been a Confucian fan.

But that's where it gets tragic.

What Confucianism emphasizes is benevolence, righteousness, propriety, wisdom, and trust; order between elders and juniors; ruler and minister, father and son. But if you studied at a top American university and got a PhD right during the heyday of the hippies, were you really going to go around teaching people to #respect teachers and revere the Way, and not engage in so much #individualism? You'd starve.

By the way, how scholars avoid starving is a bitter lesson that Confucius learned over a lifetime, and no Confucian disciple could fail to know it: in Confucius's era, great chaos was coming. He traveled from state to state hoping to find an official post and do something great. But he only knew how to recite the rites and laws of the Duke of Zhou, and didn't know how to cater to what the boss wanted, so in the end he accomplished nothing and went home. But failure is the mother of success, and his students improved a lot. Sima Qian said: "Of the seventy disciples, those who scattered and traveled among the feudal lords—the greater ones became teachers, ministers, and prime ministers; the lesser ones became friends and teachers of the scholar-officials." Why?

Because after several generations of transmission, they began to know how to read the ruler's expressions. By Mencius's time, his interactions with rulers were already somewhat different—there was talk and laughter, and he knew how to flatter the boss: "King of Qi, I see that you can't even bear to kill an ox, so you must be a benevolent ruler! Govern by the kingly way—you can do it!"

By the late Warring States period, those "Confucian" strategists had perfected the game of "interviewing with the ruler." In order to become officials, they threw away "benevolence" and "propriety" entirely, and every one of them went off to study "rule by law," this new technology. So Xunzi, as a Confucian disciple, could produce students who became high officials in the Qin Shi Huang government—in Li Si, of course, you couldn't smell even half a trace of Confucius.

Today, with more than a hundred or two hundred countries in the United Nations, the situation is more complex than in the Spring and Autumn period. Confucian heirs leave China, this (ancient) land of propriety and righteousness, and go to the barbaric land of the American military hegemony—were they really going to pull out the Analects and tell people to #observe three years of mourning? Everyone knows intimately how badly Confucius embarrassed himself when he took his students around and got the door slammed in his face. So when they go to America, Confucian disciples naturally say the Master also supported #democracy, #democracy is great, the Communist Party should be wiped out—that's how you get an Ivy League professorship!

Maybe someone asks: is there no consequence for talking nonsense? Of course not! Confucius and Mencius died more than two thousand years ago, and the Confucian tradition was smashed to pieces in the #MayFourthMovement. As a top academic authority at the only great power after World War II, if you say that Johnny Kung actually taught people to take drugs and chase women, some people might believe it too—after all, America's #theoretical nuclear bombs are the real deal, and the consequences for the Japanese when they didn't believe it are still vivid in everyone's memory.

So why was Old Yu able to casually translate a few passages from the Book of Han into English and get a PhD, and have several universities invite him to be a professor? #Youknow. When they push you onto the altar as an authority, they want you to say in dead earnest: "Everyone sitting here is a dried salted duck. You think Confucius's thought is feudal? Oh, what a huge misunderstanding! Actually, he was the first democracy fighter! If you don't have a Harvard teaching position, just sit quietly and listen!"

As for whether Confucianism conflicts with democracy—honestly, what the hell do "sinologists" know about this stuff? You say: the Republic of China is an example. Hey big shot, is Taiwan's political system really "Confucian"? Do those KMT bastards curse Tsai Ing-wen every day—is that how Confucianism works? Has Tsai Ing-wen ever put on a full show of worshipping heaven and sacrificing to the earth? No. Scientific spirit, don't be a charlatan—that violates Confucian principles. You think using Chinese characters means Confucianism? You think Confucianism can only exist in China?

As I said earlier, Japan is Confucian, Britain is Confucian (at least among the world's advanced countries, they're the most Confucian): the monarch speaks of ritual and pageantry, power is bestowed by heaven, supreme and not to be cursed; everyone is in layer upon layer of hierarchy, no one can leapfrog and challenge it—that's how society stays stable. As for the success or failure of their democratic systems, that's not something an American Chinese has the right to comment on.

---

**Commentary**

Setting aside the sarcastic, self-consciously provocative tone, the author is making a serious argument about the relationship between Confucianism and democracy—and, more pointedly, about the politics of academic authority.

The core claim is that the popular narrative that Confucianism is compatible with (or even foundational to) democracy is intellectually dishonest. The author traces a historical arc: Confucius himself was a failed official because he refused to flatter rulers; his disciples learned to adapt and flatter; by the Warring States period, "Confucian" strategists had abandoned benevolence and propriety to study the new technology of "rule by law" in order to get ahead. The implication is that Confucian tradition has always been flexible about accommodating power—and that contemporary claims about Confucianism and democracy are similarly opportunistic.

The author's real target is the way Western academia (and specifically the Ivy League) confers authority on certain Chinese intellectuals. The argument is that scholars like Yu Ying-shih were elevated not because their work was intellectually rigorous, but because they told Western audiences what they wanted to hear: that Confucianism is compatible with democracy, that Chinese tradition supports democratic values. The author suggests this is a transaction—the institution grants authority, and the scholar in return provides a legitimizing narrative. The "sinologists" don't actually understand Confucianism, the author claims; they just use it as a tool.

The essay also makes a comparative claim: that Japan and Britain are the truly "Confucian" societies, in the sense of hierarchical, ritualistic social orders—not because they follow Confucius, but because the author is using "Confucian" as a label for a certain kind of social stability. The author's point is that Confucianism is not uniquely Chinese and not inherently democratic; it is a social logic that can be found in various forms.

So the main point, stripped of sarcasm: the author is arguing that the intellectual establishment's claim that Confucianism is compatible with democracy is a self-serving fiction, that scholars who advance it are rewarded with prestige and positions, and that this is a form of intellectual dishonesty—one that the author finds both amusing and contemptible. [end of text]


